  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure that is slowly drifting east and off the Mid-Atlantic \nCoast will continue to influence the area through Tuesday. A \ndominant southerly flow has developed across the area as a result of \nthe positioning of the high, which has allowed for an increase in \nboth moisture and warm air advection into the area. Temperatures \nacross the area this morning remain mild with most locations in the \nmid to upper 50s with dewpoints in the 40s. These mild temperatures \nwill translate to highs today climbing into the mid to upper 60s. \nWith increasing moisture in the entire column, a push of mid-level \nenergy across the area today may be enough for light rain showers to \ndevelop and to reach the surface instead of evaporating. Overall \ncoverage will remain scattered with the highest confidence across \nwestern counties. By tonight, the mid-level energy will shift east \nand allow for widespread dry conditions to arrive for Tuesday. \nTuesday will see temperatures well above normal as a warm front \nlifts north across the area and temperatures surge into the 70s. \nSome areas will flirt with record setting warmth on Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA notable shift in the weather pattern will occur on Wednesday as a \ndeepening trough over the central US moves a surface low into the \nregion. This system will move a preliminary cold front east across \nthe area on Wednesday, providing a focal point for widespread \nshowers to develop. Given the persistent moisture advection into the \narea, rainfall amounts are expected to range from 0.25-0.5 inches of \nprecipitation. Given the prolonged dry period leading into this \nsystem, basins should be able to handle this amount of precipitation \nwithout problem, however in areas that see heavy rainfall there may \nbe locally increased runoff. In addition, given a strong LLJ of 40-\n50 knots pushing into the area and decent upper level support from \nthe vort max and jet, there is a non-zero chance for thunder \nWednesday afternoon but no severe weather is expected. \n\nOn Thursday, showers will gradually taper from west to east before a \nlake effect processes take over and keep shower chances across \nportions of the snowbelt into Friday. Additional shower activity is \npossible into the weekend as a series of upper level troughs pushes \neast through the region. Temperatures are expected to remain above \nnormal through the middle of the week before dipping closer to \nnormal by Friday into the weekend.",
